<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irish War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Anglo-Irish War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Irish War of Independence was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war fought by the Irish Republican Army against British forces in Ireland. It followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a>'s election victory and the declaration of independence by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a> in 1919. A truce in July 1921 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a>, which created the Irish Free State.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Irish War of Independence" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hogan%27s_Flying_Column.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since the 1870s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-parliamentary-party">Irish Parliamentary Party</a> had demanded home rule, while small groups such as Arthur Griffith's Sinn Féin argued for immediate independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of April 1916 proclaimed an Irish Republic and was put down within a week, with over four hundred dead. The execution of its leaders and the arrest of thousands of activists then raised support for Sinn Féin, and the Conscription Crisis of 1918 added to it. In the general election of 1918 Sinn Féin took 73 of the 105 Irish seats, and 91 per cent of those outside Ulster.</p><p>Its members met as the First Dáil at the Mansion House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on 21 January 1919, reaffirmed the 1916 proclamation and issued a declaration of independence. The Irish Volunteers were reconstituted as the Irish Republican Army (IRA).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> met, Seán Treacy, Séumas Robinson, Seán Hogan and Dan Breen, acting on their own initiative, shot two Royal Irish Constabulary (RIC) constables at Soloheadbeg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>. The IRA, organised in flying columns of 20 to 100 men, claimed 70,000 members but only about 3,000 were active fighters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a> built an intelligence network and a group of gunmen called the Squad.</p><p>The British reinforced the RIC with the Black and Tans, about seven thousand mostly ex-soldiers, and the Auxiliaries, who gained a reputation for indiscipline and reprisals against civilians. On Bloody Sunday, 21 November 1920, fourteen British intelligence operatives were killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and the RIC fired on a crowd at Croke Park, killing fourteen civilians. A week later the IRA killed seventeen Auxiliaries at Kilmichael. Martial law followed and the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> was burnt. About 1,000 people died between January and July 1921, and 4,500 republicans were interned. In the north-east the conflict had a sectarian aspect, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> almost 500 people were killed, most of them Catholics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A truce began on 11 July 1921 after a stalemate and growing British criticism at home and abroad. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> was signed on 6 December 1921 and ratified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> on 7 January 1922. The Irish Free State was created as a self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> on 6 December 1922, while Northern Ireland, created by the Government of Ireland Act in May 1921, remained in the United Kingdom.</p><p>One count puts the dead at 2,346: 919 civilians, 523 police, 413 British military personnel and 491 IRA volunteers. Disagreement over the Treaty among republicans led to the eleven-month Irish Civil War from June 1922.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irish War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its members met as Dáil Éireann in January 1919 and declared independence, and the Irish Republican Army fought the <b>Irish War of Independence</b> against British forces from 1919 to 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The colonized resisted: the Philippines cost the United States much more than expected because of military action against rebels, the <b>Irish War of Independence</b> of 1919–1921 created the Irish Free State, and after 1945 anti-colonial movements fought France in Vietnam and Algeria.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic built a counter-state: Dail courts, a loan raised by Michael Collins, local councils and the Irish Republican Army, which fought the <b>War of Independence</b> against British forces from 1919 to 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/irish-republic">Irish Republic</a></li></ul></section>
